{
	"schema": "kitlib-io-corpus.v1",
	"about": "One conformance corpus for kitlib io (ompkit-67i70, KL06), read by tests/cli/kitlib-io.test.ts (src/kitlib/io.ts atomicReplaceBytes) and tests/scripts/kitlib_io_test.py (scripts/kitlib_io.py atomic_replace_bytes). Each case runs in a fresh case dir DIR (realpath) inside a kitlib owned temp dir. setup entries, in order: {file, text, mode?} writes a file, {dir} makes a directory, {symlink, to} makes a symlink to the absolute path of the DIR-relative name `to`. call: path (DIR-relative; path_literal is passed verbatim), text or hex bytes, mode (integer), durability (null passes none), stage_dir and public_root (DIR-relative), umask (set around the call, restored after), validate ('accept-exact': the validator checks the temp holds exactly the bytes at the requested mode inside the stage; 'refuse': it raises). fault, through the test seam (atomicReplaceBytesWith / atomic_replace_bytes_with): temp_name (forced temp basename), write_fail {after, errno} (the first write writes `after` bytes, the next raises errno), write_stall {after} (the first write writes `after` bytes, then writes return 0), write_lie (each write writes half its bytes, rounded up, but reports all), fsync_errno, rename_errno, dir_fsync_errno (raise that errno), stage_other_device (stat of the stage reports another st_dev), hardlink_temp (right after the temp is opened, a hard link with this DIR-relative name is made to it), at {boundary, do} with do 'swap-temp' (unlink the temp and put a file holding 'attacker' at its name) or 'cancel' (set the cancellation). kind: absent for a plain call; 'concurrent-readers' (the writer publishes `size` bytes of 'a', starts reader_argv as a child, waits for the child's ready file, replaces the target `writes` times alternating `size` bytes of 'b' and 'a', then creates the stop file; the child reads the target in a loop until stop and prints `reads=N torn=M`, torn counting reads that are neither payload complete; N must be positive); 'rmw-race' (two read-modify-write increments of a decimal counter interleave: the second runs completely at the first one's temp-created boundary, with no lock). expect: ok, code (error code), published (result or error), file_synced, directory_synced, durable, cancelled_after_publish, errno (error errno name), temp_left (the error reports a temp it could not remove), files {name: text}, files_hex {name: hex}, symlinks {name: DIR-relative target}, modes {name: permission bits}, entries {dir: exact sorted names, '.' is DIR}, reader_torn, final_counter. Every case also requires the process umask and its exit/signal handlers unchanged by the call. planted_negatives: running `case` with the named detectors in `bypass` disabled must NOT meet the case's expect, while `control` with the same bypass still meets its own expect.",
	"cases": [
